// cme_lesson_narrative_text.cjs
// 5-Block Narrative Exposition Blocks for all 12 Lessons in Conflict in the Middle East (1945-1995)
// Follows the 5-Stage Chronological Inquiry Spine with decimal markers [X.1] and [X.2]

module.exports = {
  lesson_1: [
    // Stage 1: The McMahon-Hussein Correspondence (1915–1916)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[1.1]</span> For over four centuries leading up to the First World War, the lands of the Middle East were ruled as provinces of the Turkish **Ottoman Empire**. When the Ottomans entered the war on the side of Imperial Germany in October 1914, British imperial strategists faced an acute crisis: Ottoman forces threatened the vital **Suez Canal**, Britain's maritime lifeline to India. Desperate to tie down Turkish forces, Sir **Henry McMahon**, the British High Commissioner in Cairo, initiated a series of ten diplomatic letters with **Sharif Hussein of Mecca**, the custodian of Islam’s holiest shrines and leader of the Hashemite dynasty.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[1.2]</span> McMahon offered British recognition of an **independent Arab state** spanning the vast majority of the Arab world in exchange for Hussein launching an armed guerrilla rebellion against Ottoman rule. However, McMahon inserted deliberate geographic ambiguities, reserving "portions of Syria lying to the west of the districts of Damascus." Hussein understood this to mean non-Arab coastal Lebanon, believing Palestine was firmly included in the Arab sphere. Arab forces under Prince **Faisal** and T.E. Lawrence launched the **Arab Revolt** in June 1916 on the sincere belief that blood spilled in the desert guaranteed sovereign independence.`,
    },
    // Stage 2: The Secret Sykes-Picot Agreement (May 1916)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[2.1]</span> Even while McMahon was negotiating with Sharif Hussein, British and French imperial planners moved secretly to orchestrate the partition of Ottoman territory for their own benefit. In May 1916, British diplomat Sir **Mark Sykes** and French envoy **François Georges-Picot** concluded the secret **Sykes-Picot Agreement**. Using straight lines drawn across desert maps with rulers, they carved the Levant into imperial spheres of influence: France secured direct control over coastal Syria and Lebanon, while Great Britain took Mesopotamia (Iraq) and **Transjordan** to safeguard oil routes and overland communications to British India.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[2.2]</span> Recognizing the acute sensitivity of Jerusalem's Christian, Muslim, and Jewish holy sites, the negotiators designated Palestine as an **international brown zone**, to be administered jointly by Allied powers. When the Bolshevik government leaked the secret treaty in November 1917 following the Russian Revolution, Arab leaders were stunned by what they viewed as a profound imperial betrayal: European empires had partitioned sovereign Arab homelands behind closed doors while Arab guerrillas were actively fighting and dying as British allies.`,
    },
    // Stage 3: The Balfour Declaration (November 1917)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[3.1]</span> By late 1917, Britain faced immense pressure on the Western Front following the collapse of Tsarist Russia and German submarine warfare. To mobilize international Jewish diplomatic and financial influence—particularly in the United States and Russia—Prime Minister **David Lloyd George** and Foreign Secretary **Arthur Balfour** courted the British Zionist Federation. On **2 November 1917**, Balfour issued an official letter to Lord **Walter Rothschild**, proclaiming that the British Government viewed with favour "the establishment in Palestine of a national home for the Jewish people."`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[3.2]</span> To appease internal critics and Arab allies, Balfour added a famous safeguard clause: "nothing shall be done which may prejudice the civil and religious rights of existing non-Jewish communities." At the time, Arab Muslims and Christians formed over **90% of the population** of Palestine, while Jews comprised fewer than 10%. By promising to foster a national Jewish home in an Arab-majority land while pledging not to harm Arab rights, the British Cabinet created a fatal, irreconcilable contradiction that would inflame communal conflict for decades.`,
    },
    // Stage 4: The British Mandate & Rising Immigration (1920–1936)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[4.1]</span> Following Allied military conquest and General **Edmund Allenby's** entrance into Jerusalem in December 1917, the newly formed **League of Nations** ratified the Mandate System at the 1920 San Remo Conference. Under Article 22 of the League Covenant, Britain was granted formal administrative governance over **Mandatory Palestine**, incorporating the Balfour commitments directly into international law. The **Zionist Organization**, led by **Chaim Weizmann**, established the **Jewish Agency** to coordinate immigration and purchase land through the **Jewish National Fund** (JNF).`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[4.2]</span> For indigenous Palestinian Arabs, rapid Jewish immigration represented an existential threat of imperial dispossession. When Zionist agencies purchased large agricultural estates from absentee Ottoman landlords in Beirut and Damascus, Arab tenant farmers (**fellahin**) were evicted from lands their families had cultivated for generations. Communal tensions erupted into deadly violence during the **1921 Jaffa riots** and the **1929 Wailing Wall riots**, which claimed 133 Jewish and 116 Arab lives. As European fascism rose in the 1930s, Jewish immigration surged, expanding the Jewish population from 11% in 1922 to nearly **30% by 1936**.`,
    },
    // Stage 5: The Arab Revolt & The 1939 White Paper (1936–1939)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[5.1]</span> In April 1936, simmering Arab anger erupted into the **Great Arab Revolt**. Led by the Arab Higher Committee under the Grand Mufti of Jerusalem, **Haj Amin al-Husseini**, Palestinian Arabs launched a six-month general strike, withholding taxes and boycotting British goods. When strikes failed to halt Jewish immigration, the rebellion transformed into a countryside peasant guerrilla war, attacking British military convoys, sabotaging the **IPC oil pipeline** to Haifa, and targeting Jewish agricultural settlements (**kibbutzim**). Britain responded with ruthless martial law, deploying 20,000 troops, demolishing rebel homes, and executing Arab leaders.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[5.2]</span> With the Second World War looming and the British Empire desperately needing to secure Arab oil resources and trade routes against Nazi Germany, Colonial Secretary Malcolm MacDonald published the **1939 White Paper**. Reversing previous partition proposals, the White Paper strictly capped Jewish immigration to **75,000 over five years**, conditioned any further entry on Arab consent, and promised independent statehood within ten years under majority rule. Zionist leaders were outraged, condemning the policy as a death sentence for European Jewry on the eve of the **Holocaust** and vowing to resist British rule.`,
    },
  ],

  lesson_2: [
    // Stage 1: Holocaust Survivors & The 1,500 Monthly Limit (1945–1946)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[1.1]</span> In the summer of 1945, the liberation of Nazi concentration camps confronted the world with the horrific slaughter of six million European Jews. Over 250,000 traumatised Jewish survivors remained stranded in European **Displaced Persons (DP) camps**, refusing to return to homes destroyed by pogroms and demanding passage to Palestine. US President **Harry S. Truman** exerted intense diplomatic pressure on Britain to immediately admit 100,000 Holocaust survivors into the Mandate.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[1.2]</span> However, British Foreign Secretary **Ernest Bevin** adamantly rejected Truman's appeal, fearing that a massive influx would ignite an all-out Arab uprising that would jeopardize Middle Eastern oil supplies and air transit routes. Bevin strictly enforced the **1939 White Paper quota**, capping Jewish entry at a mere **1,500 immigrants per month**. The Royal Navy intercepted refugee ships at sea and deported thousands of intercepted survivors to internment camps behind barbed wire on Cyprus, provoking international outrage.`,
    },
    // Stage 2: The Zionist Paramilitary Insurgency (Nov 1945 – 1946)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[2.1]</span> Convinced that British immigration restrictions condemned survivors to indefinite confinement, the three major Zionist underground paramilitaries united in November 1945 to form the **United Resistance Movement** (**Tenuat HaMeri**). The mainstream **Haganah** (led by David Ben-Gurion), the right-wing **Irgun Zvai Leumi** (commanded by Menachem Begin), and the radical **Lehi** (Stern Gang) launched a coordinated insurgency to paralyze British security infrastructure.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[2.2]</span> In June 1946, the Haganah launched the devastating **Night of the Bridges**, blowing up ten of the eleven bridges linking Palestine to neighbouring Arab states in a single evening. The British responded on 29 June with **Operation Agatha** ("Black Saturday"), deploying 17,000 troops, imposing nationwide curfews, raiding the Jewish Agency headquarters, and arresting over 2,700 Zionist leaders. This crackdown convinced the Irgun that British authority in Palestine had to be shattered through direct urban warfare.`,
    },
    // Stage 3: The King David Hotel Bombing (22 July 1946)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[3.1]</span> On **22 July 1946**, the Irgun executed a catastrophic strike against the administrative nerve-centre of the British Mandate. Six Irgun operatives disguised as Arab milk deliverymen slipped into the basement of the **King David Hotel** in Jerusalem, which housed the British Military Headquarters and Civil Secretariat. They rolled milk churns packed with 350 kilograms of explosives beneath the building's south-west support pillars.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[3.2]</span> The thunderous blast collapsed the entire six-storey southern wing into rubble, killing **91 people**—including 28 British administrative officials, 41 Arab civil servants, 17 Jewish employees, and 5 bystanders. The massacre horrified world opinion and caused the mainstream Haganah to withdraw from the United Resistance Movement. For Britain, the bombing proved Palestine had become ungovernable without full-scale military occupation, forcing British personnel into heavily fortified, barbed-wire security enclaves derisively called "**Bevingrads**."`,
    },
    // Stage 4: The Sergeants Affair & Public Backlash (July 1947)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[4.1]</span> In July 1947, after British military courts sentenced three captured Irgun members to death for the Acre Prison break, Menachem Begin's underground kidnapped two off-duty British military intelligence sergeants, **Clifford Martin** and **Mervyn Paice**, in Netanya. Begin warned that the soldiers would be executed if the British carried out the death sentences. When the British hanged the three Irgun prisoners at Acre on 29 July, the Irgun executed the two British sergeants.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[4.2]</span> The Irgun hanged the sergeants' bodies in an orange grove and booby-trapped Martin's corpse with an improvised landmine, which severely wounded a British army officer attempting to cut the body down. The **Sergeants Affair** triggered a fierce backlash across mainland Britain: anti-Semitic riots erupted in London, Liverpool, and Manchester, while British newspapers demanded an immediate troop withdrawal. British taxpayers, exhausted by post-war rationing, questioned why £40 million a year and 100,000 soldiers were being wasted policing Palestine.`,
    },
    // Stage 5: The SS Exodus Crisis & Handover to the UN (Summer–Nov 1947)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[5.1]</span> In July 1947, the Haganah launched its boldest **Aliyah Bet** clandestine immigration effort with the **SS Exodus 1947**, carrying 4,515 Holocaust survivors, including 655 children. When the Royal Navy intercepted the unseaworthy vessel in international waters, boarding parties stormed the ship with tear gas and batons, killing three refugees. Rather than detaining the passengers in Cyprus, Ernest Bevin ordered the survivors shipped back to France; when they refused to disembark, Britain forcibly returned them to transit camps in Hamburg, **Germany**.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[5.2]</span> Images of British soldiers using batons to force Holocaust survivors onto German soil beneath barbed wire caused a catastrophic international public relations disaster for Britain. The newly arrived **UNSCOP** (UN Special Committee on Palestine) delegation witnessed the crisis in Haifa harbour first-hand. Realizing that the Mandate was bleeding British finances, alienating the United States, and hopelessly entangling British soldiers, Prime Minister Clement Attlee’s Cabinet officially surrendered the Palestine problem to the **United Nations** in February 1947.`,
    },
  ],

  lesson_3: [
    // Stage 1: The UN Ceasefire & The Czech Arms Supply (June–July 1948)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[1.1]</span> When five Arab armies invaded Mandatory Palestine on 15 May 1948, the fledgling State of Israel was in a precarious military position, facing acute shortages of heavy artillery, ammunition, and combat aircraft. On **11 June 1948**, UN mediator **Count Folke Bernadotte** negotiated a four-week truce. While the Arab League respected the truce and suffered from a British arms embargo, David Ben-Gurion utilized the pause to reorganize disparate paramilitary militias into a unified, disciplined national army: the **Israel Defense Forces** (IDF).`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[1.2]</span> Crucially, Israel breached the international UN arms embargo through "**Operation Balak**," purchasing thousands of modern rifles, machine guns, and 25 **Avia S-199 fighter aircraft** from Communist **Czechoslovakia** with secret approval from Soviet leader Joseph Stalin. When fighting resumed on 8 July during the "**Ten Days**," the IDF deployed modern fighter aircraft and tanks, shattering Arab offensives, capturing western Galilee, and seizing strategic initiative across all operational fronts.`,
    },
    // Stage 2: The Palestinian Refugee Crisis (The Nakba) (1948–1949)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[2.1]</span> The 1948–49 War resulted in an unprecedented humanitarian catastrophe for Palestinian Arab society, an event known in Arabic as **Al-Nakba** ("The Catastrophe"). Between December 1947 and the 1949 armistices, over **700,000 Palestinian Arabs**—representing over 80% of the Arab population living within the borders of what became Israel—fled or were expelled from their ancestral towns and villages. Over **400 Arab villages** were systematically evacuated, abandoned, or razed.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[2.2]</span> Historians document that the refugee exodus was multi-causal: panic spread following atrocities like the **Deir Yassin massacre** in April 1948; Israeli military commanders executed deliberate expulsions under **Plan Dalet** (most notoriously in the mass expulsion of 50,000 residents from **Lydda and Ramle**); and Arab administrative collapse left civilians leaderless. The displaced population was forced into emergency refugee camps in the Gaza Strip, West Bank, Jordan, Lebanon, and Syria, where families remained stateless and dependent on international aid.`,
    },
    // Stage 3: The 1949 Armistice Agreements (The Green Line) (1949)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[3.1]</span> In early 1949, on the Mediterranean island of Rhodes, UN acting mediator **Ralph Bunche** brokered separate bilateral armistice agreements between Israel and Egypt, Lebanon, Jordan, and Syria. The resulting demarcation boundaries were drawn with green grease-pencils on military maps, becoming internationally known as the **Green Line**. Crucially, the Arab delegations refused to sign permanent peace treaties or recognize Israeli statehood, insisting that the Green Line represented purely temporary military ceasefire lines.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[3.2]</span> The 1949 Green Line dramatically redrew the map of the Middle East. Israel secured **78% of Mandatory Palestine**—a 22% increase over the territorial allocation granted by UN Resolution 181. Transjordan annexed the **West Bank** and the Old City of East Jerusalem, renaming itself the Hashemite Kingdom of Jordan, while Egypt retained military administration over the crowded **Gaza Strip**. No independent Palestinian Arab state emerged, leaving Palestinians entirely disenfranchised.`,
    },
    // Stage 4: The Israeli Law of Return & Demographic Growth (July 1950)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[4.1]</span> On **5 July 1950**, the Israeli Knesset enacted the historic **Law of Return**, declaring that every Jewish person anywhere in the world possessed the inherent legal right to settle in Israel and receive automatic citizenship. Within four years of statehood, Israel’s Jewish population more than doubled, surging from 650,000 in May 1948 to over **1.4 million by 1952**. This massive influx transformed the demographic security and military mobilization capacity of the young republic.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[4.2]</span> The demographic wave arrived in two distinct streams: European Holocaust survivors followed by over 300,000 **Mizrahi and Sephardic Jews** fleeing persecution, riots, and property seizures in Arab countries (including Iraq, Yemen, Morocco, and Egypt). Absorbing these impoverished refugees created severe economic hardship: food was strictly rationed (**Tzena**), and families were housed in squalid tin-shack transit camps (**Ma'abarot**) until West German Holocaust reparations (**Wiedergutmachung**) and US loans stabilized the national economy.`,
    },
    // Stage 5: Fedayeen Border Raids & Israeli Reprisals (1950–1955)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[5.1]</span> The 1949 Green Line was an arbitrary, unfortified border running through agricultural valleys and village orchards. Displaced Palestinian refugees in Gaza and the West Bank frequently crossed the armistice line to harvest crops, recover livestock, or visit relatives. Israeli authorities viewed all unauthorized crossings as hostile infiltration. By 1953, Egyptian military intelligence in Gaza began organizing, arming, and financing these infiltrators into organized guerrilla squads known as **fedayeen** ("self-sacrificers"), who carried out sabotage, planted landmines, and attacked Israeli border settlements.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[5.2]</span> In response, Prime Minister David Ben-Gurion instituted an aggressive military doctrine of disproportionate retaliation, arguing that only crushing military counter-strikes could deter Arab aggression. In August 1953, the IDF formed **Unit 101**, an elite commando unit commanded by Major **Ariel Sharon**. In October 1953, following the murder of an Israeli mother and children in Yehud, Unit 101 raided the Jordanian border village of **Qibya**, blowing up 45 houses and killing **69 Arab civilians**, drawing stern international condemnation from the UN Security Council.`,
    },
  ],

  lesson_4: [
    // Stage 1: The Gaza Raid (February 1955)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[1.1]</span> On the night of **28 February 1955**, Israeli paratroopers commanded by Ariel Sharon carried out **Operation Black Arrow**, a massive cross-border assault against an Egyptian military garrison in the Gaza Strip. The Israeli commando raid was exceptionally destructive: **37 Egyptian soldiers** and two civilians were killed, while Egyptian barracks and a railway water pump station were demolished with explosives.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[1.2]</span> The Gaza Raid proved a decisive turning point in Middle Eastern history. It completely shattered Egyptian President **Gamal Abdel Nasser’s** domestic military prestige and demonstrated the catastrophic technological inferiority of Egyptian forces compared to the IDF. Humiliated by the defeat, Nasser realized that Western powers—bound by the 1950 Tripartite Declaration—refused to supply Egypt with heavy offensive weaponry, compelling him to seek an alternative superpower alliance.`,
    },
    // Stage 2: The Czech Arms Deal (September 1955)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[2.1]</span> Rebuffed by the United States and Britain, Nasser turned to the Soviet Union. In **September 1955**, Egypt announced the **Czech Arms Deal**—a massive \$250 million arms agreement ostensibly negotiated with Czechoslovakia but fully backed by Moscow. In exchange for Egyptian cotton, the Soviet bloc delivered over **200 MiG-15 jet fighters**, 50 Ilyushin bombers, 300 modern T-34 and JS-3 tanks, 200 armoured troop carriers, and two naval submarines.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[2.2]</span> The Czech Arms Deal caused intense alarm in Western capitals and Tel Aviv. It demolished the Western monopoly on Middle Eastern armaments, shattered the regional balance of power, and established the Soviet Union as a dominant geopolitical player in the Arab world. In Israel, military planners feared that once Egyptian pilots and tank crews completed Soviet training, Egypt would launch an annihilating war against the Jewish state.`,
    },
    // Stage 3: Dam Loans Cancelled & Suez Canal Nationalised (July 1956)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[3.1]</span> Nasser's crowning domestic development project was the **Aswan High Dam**, designed to control the annual Nile flooding, irrigate millions of acres of desert, and provide hydroelectric power for Egyptian industrialization. Angered by Nasser's recognition of Communist China and his Soviet arms purchases, US Secretary of State **John Foster Dulles** abruptly cancelled American and World Bank loan financing in **July 1956**, believing this would humiliate Nasser. Britain immediately followed suit.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[3.2]</span> On **26 July 1956**, in a fiery, defiant address before a crowd of 250,000 in Alexandria, Nasser gave the secret codename "de Lesseps" to Egyptian troops, who seized the offices of the **Suez Canal Company**. Nasser nationalized the waterway, announcing that transit tolls collected from Western shipping would directly finance the Aswan Dam. British Prime Minister **Anthony Eden** and French leaders viewed the nationalisation as an intolerable threat to imperial communications and Persian Gulf oil supplies.`,
    },
    // Stage 4: The Secret Sèvres Plot & Invasion (October–November 1956)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[4.1]</span> Between 22 and 24 October 1956, senior British, French, and Israeli officials met covertly at a private villa in **Sèvres**, France. Anthony Eden, French Prime Minister Guy Mollet, and Israeli Prime Minister David Ben-Gurion signed the secret **Protocol of Sèvres**, orchestrating a cynical military conspiracy: Israel would launch a surprise invasion of the Sinai Peninsula toward the Suez Canal; Britain and France would then issue an ultimatum commanding both sides to withdraw ten miles from the waterway and intervene to "separate the combatants."`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[4.2]</span> On **29 October 1956**, Israeli paratroopers under Ariel Sharon dropped near the **Mitla Pass**, while Israeli armoured columns surged across the Sinai. On 30 October, London and Paris issued their pre-planned ultimatum, which Nasser predictably rejected. On 31 October, Anglo-French air forces bombed Egyptian airfields, destroying Nasser's air force, before dropping British paratroopers and Royal Marine commandos to seize **Port Said** on 5 November.`,
    },
    // Stage 5: US Ultimatum & British Retreat (November 1956)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[5.1]</span> The Anglo-French conspiracy provoked incandescent fury from US President **Dwight D. Eisenhower**, who was running for re-election on a platform of peace and had been kept completely in the dark. Eisenhower warned that imperial aggression in Egypt undermined Western moral authority just as the Soviet Union was violently crushing the Hungarian Uprising. When Soviet Premier Bulganin threatened rocket attacks against London and Paris, Eisenhower refused to support Britain, threatening to collapse the British pound by blocking IMF emergency loans unless all forces withdrew.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[5.2]</span> Facing total financial bankruptcy and run on sterling, Eden declared a humiliating ceasefire on **6 November 1956**. The newly established **United Nations Emergency Force (UNEF)** deployed to Sinai to supervise the withdrawal of Anglo-French and Israeli forces. The **Suez Crisis** marked the definitive end of Britain and France as global imperial superpowers, forced Eden's resignation, opened the Straits of Tiran to Israeli shipping under UN supervision, and elevated Nasser into the undisputed hero of **Pan-Arab nationalism**.`,
    },
  ],

  lesson_6: [
    // Stage 1: The 1964 Cairo Conference & PLO Founded (January 1964)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[1.1]</span> In **January 1964**, Egyptian President Gamal Abdel Nasser convened the **First Arab League Summit** in Cairo, bringing together thirteen heads of state to forge a unified Arab strategy against Israel. Arab leaders were particularly alarmed by Israel's completion of the **National Water Carrier**, a massive infrastructure project pumping water from the Sea of Galilee across the coastal plain to irrigate the Negev desert, which Arab states viewed as an illegal seizure of vital regional water resources.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[1.2]</span> Under Nasser's direct sponsorship, the Cairo Summit formally authorized the creation of the **Palestine Liberation Organisation** (**PLO**), establishing the Palestinian National Charter and creating the Palestine Liberation Army (PLA). Led initially by Ahmad Shukeiri, the PLO declared that armed struggle was the only path to liberate Palestine. While Nasser intended to keep the PLO under strict Egyptian control, independent guerrilla factions like **Yasser Arafat's Fatah** soon began executing cross-border armed operations.`,
    },
    // Stage 2: The Dispute Over the River Jordan Waterways (1964–1965)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[2.1]</span> In response to Israel's National Water Carrier, Arab states initiated the **Headwaters Diversion Plan** in late 1964. Engineers in Syria, Lebanon, and Jordan began constructing vast channels and canals to divert the **Hasbani and Banias rivers**—the primary northern tributaries of the Jordan River—away from the Sea of Galilee into the Yarmouk River in Jordan, cutting Israel’s water intake by **35%**.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[2.2]</span> Israel declared that any unilateral diversion of its freshwater supply constituted an act of war. In 1965 and 1966, Israeli tank gunners commanded by General **Israel Tal** and Israeli Air Force Mirages conducted precision strikes across the armistice line, destroying Syrian earth-moving equipment, dams, and diversionary canals along the rocky slopes of the **Golan Heights**. The Water Wars brought the Syrian-Israeli frontier to the brink of open warfare.`,
    },
    // Stage 3: Syrian Support for Fatah Border Raids (1966)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[3.1]</span> In February 1966, a radical left-wing military coup within Syria brought the militant **Neo-Ba'athist** faction to power in Damascus, led by Salah Jadid and Defence Minister **Hafez al-Assad**. The new regime openly embraced a doctrine of "popular liberation war," establishing Damascus as the operational headquarters and training ground for Yasser Arafat's **Fatah** guerrillas.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[3.2]</span> Armed with Soviet weaponry, Fatah commandos crossed into northern Israel via Lebanon and the Jordanian West Bank, planting landmines on agricultural roads, sabotaging water pumping stations, and attacking civilian rail lines. From fortified artillery bunkers atop the **Golan Heights**, Syrian gunners regularly shelled Israeli farming kibbutzim in the Hula Valley below, prompting furious warnings from Israeli Prime Minister **Levi Eshkol** that Syria would face devastating military retribution.`,
    },
    // Stage 4: The Israeli Raid on Samu (13 November 1966)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[4.1]</span> On **13 November 1966**, following a Fatah landmine attack that killed three Israeli border police near the southern West Bank border, Israel launched its largest military operation since the Suez Crisis: **Operation Shredder**, targeting the Jordanian-controlled village of **As-Samu**. Israeli Centurion tanks and paratroopers crossed the armistice line, expecting little resistance from the Jordanian military.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[4.2]</span> However, a Jordanian infantry battalion counter-attacked, resulting in a fierce pitched battle. Israeli forces destroyed dozens of houses and a clinic; **15 Jordanian soldiers** and three civilians were killed, and 54 were wounded. The Samu Raid severely undermined Jordan’s pro-Western **King Hussein**, triggered mass anti-government riots in Amman, and led Arab critics to accuse Nasser of hiding behind the UN peacekeepers (UNEF) in Sinai while Jordanian Arabs were slaughtered.`,
    },
    // Stage 5: The Aerial Battle of 7 April 1967 (7 April 1967)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[5.1]</span> On **7 April 1967**, tensions along the Syrian border reached an explosive climax. An Israeli armoured tractor was deployed into the disputed demilitarised zone to cultivate land, deliberately drawing Syrian artillery fire from the Golan Heights. In response, the Israeli Air Force scrambled French-built **Mirage III** fighter jets to bomb Syrian artillery positions.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[5.2]</span> When Syrian Air Force **MiG-21** fighters rose to intercept them, Israeli pilots shot down **six Syrian MiGs** in a succession of dogfights over Lake Tiberias. Victorious Israeli Mirages flew defiant victory loops directly over the Syrian capital of **Damascus** before returning to base. This humiliating rout convinced Syrian leaders that an Israeli invasion to overthrow the Ba'ath regime was imminent, setting the stage for the fatal Soviet intelligence deceptions of May 1967.`,
    },
  ],

  lesson_7: [
    // Stage 1: Soviet False Reports & Egyptian Mobilisation (13–15 May 1967)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[1.1]</span> On **13 May 1967**, the Soviet Union delivered an urgent intelligence briefing to Egyptian President Gamal Abdel Nasser, falsely warning that Israel had concentrated **ten to twelve combat brigades** on the Syrian border and planned an immediate invasion to overthrow the Ba'athist government in Damascus. Although UN observers and US intelligence confirmed that no Israeli troop buildup existed, Nasser felt politically compelled to act.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[1.2]</span> Stung by Arab taunts that he was hiding behind United Nations peacekeepers, Nasser ordered two Egyptian infantry divisions and hundreds of tanks to march openly through Cairo on **14–15 May**, crossing the Suez Canal into the Sinai Peninsula. By deploying massive forces directly onto Israel’s southern border, Nasser sought to reassert his leadership over the Arab world, setting off an unstoppable chain reaction toward war.`,
    },
    // Stage 2: UN Peacekeepers Expelled & Straits of Tiran Closed (16–23 May 1967)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[2.1]</span> On **16 May 1967**, Nasser demanded that UN Secretary-General U Thant immediately withdraw the **United Nations Emergency Force (UNEF)** peacekeepers, who had separated Egyptian and Israeli forces in Sinai since the 1956 Suez Crisis. U Thant acceded without consulting the Security Council, stripping away the international buffer and leaving Egyptian and Israeli armies eyeball-to-eyeball across the desert frontier.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[2.2]</span> On **22–23 May**, Nasser took the fatal step: he closed the narrow **Straits of Tiran** at Sharm el-Sheikh to all Israeli shipping and strategic goods heading to the port of Eilat. Israel had declared in 1957 that blockading the Straits would be treated as an explicit **casus belli** (act of war). In fiery speeches broadcast across the Middle East on the "Voice of the Arabs," Nasser declared: "The Jews threaten war; we say to them: Welcome, we are ready for war!"`,
    },
    // Stage 3: The Arab Defence Pact & Encirclement of Israel (30 May 1967)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[3.1]</span> On **30 May 1967**, in a dramatic reconciliation, Jordan’s **King Hussein** flew to Cairo and signed a mutual **Joint Defence Pact** with his bitter rival Nasser, placing the Royal Jordanian Army under Egyptian operational command. Days later, Iraq joined the military coalition, dispatching infantry and armoured brigades toward the Jordanian border.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[3.2]</span> For Israel, this three-front encirclement represented an existential threat: Arab armies mobilized over 250,000 troops, 2,000 tanks, and 700 combat aircraft along Israel’s borders. With Israel’s waistline measuring barely nine miles wide between the West Bank and the Mediterranean Sea, civilian populations dug air-raid trenches, school basements were converted into shelters, and parks were consecrated as emergency cemeteries during the agonizing three-week "Waiting Period" (**Hamtana**).`,
    },
    // Stage 4: Operation Focus & The Pre-Emptive Air Strike (5 June 1967)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[4.1]</span> At 07:45 on Monday, **5 June 1967**, the Israeli Air Force launched **Operation Focus** (**Mivtza Moked**), masterminded by IAF Commander **Mordechai Hod**. Nearly 200 Israeli combat aircraft flew low under Arab radar over the Mediterranean Sea, maintaining strict radio silence, and struck eleven Egyptian airbases just as Egyptian pilots finished their morning patrols and commanders were stuck in Cairo traffic.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[4.2]</span> Utilizing specialized French runway-cratering bombs, Israeli jets pulverized tarmac runways, trapping Egyptian aircraft before destroying them with cannon fire. Within three hours, the IAF annihilated **over 300 Egyptian combat aircraft** on the ground. Later that afternoon, when Syrian, Jordanian, and Iraqi air forces attacked, Israeli jets eliminated another 100 enemy planes. By sunset on 5 June, Israel had established absolute **air supremacy**, effectively deciding the outcome of the war on its very first morning.`,
    },
    // Stage 5: The Six-Day Victory & The Conquered Territories (5–10 June 1967)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[5.1]</span> With air control secured, Israeli armoured divisions commanded by Generals **Israel Tal**, **Avraham Yoffe**, and **Ariel Sharon** sliced through Egyptian defensive lines in Sinai, routing seven Egyptian divisions and trapping retreating armour at the **Mitla Pass** before reaching the Suez Canal in four days. On the eastern front, after Jordanian artillery shelled West Jerusalem, Israeli paratroopers broke through Jordanian fortifications at **Ammunition Hill**, capturing the Old City of Jerusalem and the entire West Bank by 7 June.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[5.2]</span> On 9 June, Israeli forces turned north to scale the formidable basalt escarpments of the **Golan Heights**, capturing the Syrian plateau by 10 June when a UN ceasefire halted hostilities. In six stunning days, Israel had defeated three Arab armies, captured the **Sinai Peninsula, the Gaza Strip, the West Bank, East Jerusalem, and the Golan Heights**, quadrupled its territorial size, and brought over **one million Palestinian Arabs** under military occupation.`,
    },
  ],

  lesson_8: [
    // Stage 1: The Capture & Annexation of East Jerusalem (7 June 1967)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[1.1]</span> On the morning of **7 June 1967**, Israeli paratroopers commanded by Colonel **Motta Gur** stormed through the Lion's Gate into the Old City of Jerusalem, broadcasting the famous message: "The Temple Mount is in our hands!" For the first time since the 1948 war, Jews regained access to their holiest sanctuary, the **Western Wall** (**Kotel**). Defence Minister **Moshe Dayan** and General **Yitzhak Rabin** joined ecstatic troops praying at the ancient stones.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[1.2]</span> Within three weeks, the Knesset passed legislation officially dissolving the municipal boundaries and unilaterally annexing **East Jerusalem** into the State of Israel, declaring the unified city the "eternal and indivisible capital" of the Jewish people. This annexation provoked immediate international outrage; the UN General Assembly voted 99 to 0 condemning the annexation and affirming that territorial acquisition by war is inadmissible under international law.`,
    },
    // Stage 2: The Khartoum Summit & The "Three Noes" (September 1967)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[2.1]</span> Stunned by their catastrophic defeat, eight Arab heads of state convened in the Sudanese capital of **Khartoum** in late August 1967 to forge a collective post-war strategy. Moderates like King Hussein of Jordan sought diplomatic compromise to recover lost territory, but radical regimes in Syria and Algeria demanded continued military resistance and an economic oil boycott against Western allies of Israel.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[2.2]</span> On **1 September 1967**, the summit issued the famous **Khartoum Resolution**, defined by the uncompromising "**Three Noes**": **No peace with Israel, no recognition of Israel, no negotiations with Israel**. By rejecting direct diplomacy or territorial compromise, the Khartoum Summit hardened Israeli political opinion, convincing Israeli leaders that Arab states remained committed to Israel's destruction and eliminating prospects for an immediate post-war peace deal.`,
    },
    // Stage 3: UN Resolution 242: "Land for Peace" (22 November 1967)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[3.1]</span> After months of intense diplomatic wrangling, the United Nations Security Council unanimously adopted **Resolution 242** on **22 November 1967**. Drafted by British Ambassador Lord Caradon, the resolution established the foundational principle of Middle Eastern diplomacy: "**Land for Peace**." It called for the withdrawal of Israeli armed forces from "territories occupied in the recent conflict" in exchange for the termination of all claims of belligerency and respect for the sovereignty of every state in the region.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[3.2]</span> However, Resolution 242 contained a deliberate linguistic ambiguity that became a source of permanent dispute: the English text called for withdrawal from "**territories occupied**" (leaving room for minor border adjustments), whereas the French text specified "**des territoires occupés**" (implying all territories). Crucially, the resolution referred to Palestinian Arabs only as a generic "refugee problem," completely ignoring their national right to self-determination and prompting the PLO to reject the resolution entirely.`,
    },
    // Stage 4: The Bar-Lev Line on the Suez Canal (1968–1969)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[4.1]</span> Determined to hold the newly conquered Sinai Peninsula as a strategic defensive shield, the IDF constructed a massive chain of fortifications along the eastern bank of the Suez Canal: the **Bar-Lev Line**, named after Chief of Staff **Haim Bar-Lev**. Costing \$300 million, the defensive system consisted of a gigantic **20-metre high artificial sand rampart** sloping into the canal at a 45-degree angle, backed by 35 heavily reinforced concrete bunker complexes (**maozim**).`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[4.2]</span> The bunkers were designed to withstand heavy artillery fire, equipped with machine-gun nests, minefields, and an underwater pipe system intended to pump crude oil into the canal to ignite a flaming barrier against amphibious assaults. Israeli military strategists believed the Bar-Lev Line was impregnable, breeding a dangerous sense of military complacency known as the "**Conception**" (**Ha-Kontseptsiya**)—the dogma that Arab armies lacked the military capability to cross the canal.`,
    },
    // Stage 5: The Beginning of Israeli Settlements (1968–1973)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[5.1]</span> In the immediate aftermath of the Six-Day War, Israeli Deputy Prime Minister **Yigal Allon** presented a strategic blueprint known as the **Allon Plan**. It proposed retaining a permanent security perimeter along the Jordan River valley and the Judaean Desert to serve as a military buffer against eastern invasion, while returning densely populated Arab hill country to Jordanian administrative control in exchange for peace.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[5.2]</span> Under the Allon framework, the Israeli government established strategic agricultural outposts (**Nahal**) along the Jordan Valley and Golan Heights. Concurrently, ideological and religious Zionists—later organized as **Gush Emunim** ("Bloc of the Faithful")—began establishing unauthorized outposts in the biblical heartlands of Judea and Samaria, such as Kfar Etzion and Hebron. These early settlements transformed over time from temporary security garrisons into permanent suburban townships, severely complicating future territorial compromise.`,
    },
  ],

  lesson_9: [
    // Stage 1: The Battle of Karameh (21 March 1968)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[1.1]</span> Following the humiliation of regular Arab armies in the Six-Day War, armed Palestinian resistance factions assumed direct leadership of the struggle. On **21 March 1968**, the IDF launched a massive cross-border punitive expedition against a Fatah guerrilla stronghold in the Jordanian border town of **Karameh**, deploying armour, paratroopers, and airstrikes to eliminate commando bases.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[1.2]</span> Unlike previous raids where guerrillas retreated, Fatah fighters led by **Yasser Arafat** stood and fought alongside Jordanian regular army artillery. While Israel achieved its tactical objective, destroying the camp, it suffered **28 dead** and lost four tanks. Across the Arab world, Karameh was celebrated as a glorious moral victory proving that Palestinian fedayeen could face the IDF. Thousands of young volunteers rushed to join Fatah, and in 1969 Arafat was elected Chairman of the **PLO**.`,
    },
    // Stage 2: The Dawson’s Field Airline Hijackings (September 1970)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[2.1]</span> Frustrated by conventional military limits, radical Marxist factions of the PLO, notably **George Habash's Popular Front for the Liberation of Palestine** (**PFLP**), adopted international aircraft hijackings to thrust the Palestinian cause onto world television screens. On **6 September 1970**, PFLP commandos hijacked four Western airliners in mid-flight across Europe.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[2.2]</span> Three passenger jets—a Swissair DC-8, a TWA Boeing 707, and later a BOAC VC10—were forced to land at **Dawson's Field**, a remote desert airstrip in Jordan. The hijackers held over **300 international passengers hostage**, segregated Jewish passengers, and demanded the release of Palestinian militants imprisoned in European and Israeli jails. On 12 September, after evacuating the hostages, the PFLP blew up the multi-million-dollar airliners in front of international news cameras, directly humiliating Jordan's King Hussein.`,
    },
    // Stage 3: Black September (Civil War in Jordan) (September 1970)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[3.1]</span> By late 1970, armed PLO guerrillas operated in Jordan as an autonomous "**state-within-a-state**." Uniformed fedayeen carried weapons openly in Amman, collected illegal taxes, set up roadblocks, and even attempted to assassinate King Hussein twice. The Dawson's Field spectacle was the final straw for the Jordanian monarchy, threatening the sovereignty of the Hashemite kingdom.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[3.2]</span> On **16 September 1970**, King Hussein declared martial law and ordered the Royal Jordanian Army to crush the Palestinian armed enclaves in a brutal military campaign known as **Black September**. Jordanian tanks shelled refugee camps in Amman and Irbid; after Syrian armoured intervention was repelled by the Jordanian Air Force, the PLO suffered thousands of casualties. By July 1971, King Hussein had completely expelled the armed PLO leadership and fighters from Jordan, forcing them to relocate their headquarters to **southern Lebanon**.`,
    },
    // Stage 4: The Munich Olympics Massacre (5–6 September 1972)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[4.1]</span> In the early morning hours of **5 September 1972**, eight commandos belonging to a clandestine Fatah-linked terrorist cell calling itself **Black September** infiltrated the Olympic Village at the Munich Games in West Germany. Armed with assault rifles and grenades, they stormed the quarters of the Israeli Olympic delegation at 31 Connollystraße, killing wrestling coach Moshe Weinberg and weightlifter Yossef Romano, and taking **nine Israeli athletes and coaches hostage**.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[4.2]</span> The terrorists demanded the immediate release of 234 Palestinian prisoners in Israel and German radicals Andreas Baader and Ulrike Meinhof. That night, during a botched, chaotic rescue attempt by Bavarian police at the **Fürstenfeldbruck airfield**, the terrorists detonated a hand grenade inside a helicopter and shot the bound hostages. All **eleven Israeli athletes**, five terrorists, and one German police officer were killed, horrifying a live global television audience of 900 million viewers.`,
    },
    // Stage 5: Israeli Retaliation (Operation Wrath of God) (1972–1979)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[5.1]</span> The Munich massacre galvanized Israeli Prime Minister **Golda Meir** and Defence Minister Moshe Dayan to authorize a covert global assassination campaign: **Operation Wrath of God** (**Mivtza Za'am Ha'El**). A secret Mossad executive unit known as "Committee X" dispatched assassination squads across Europe and the Middle East to eliminate every operative involved in the planning of the Munich attack.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[5.2]</span> Over seven years, Mossad agents assassinated more than a dozen Palestinian figures using car bombs, silenced pistols, and telephone booby-traps in Rome, Paris, Nicosia, and Beirut. In April 1973, Israeli commandos led by **Ehud Barak** (disguised as a woman) conducted a daring raid into downtown Beirut (**Operation Spring of Youth**), killing three senior PLO leaders in their apartments. While the campaign disrupted international terror networks, it suffered a humiliating blow in July 1973 during the **Lillehammer Affair** in Norway, when Mossad agents mistakenly murdered an innocent Moroccan waiter, Ahmed Bouchikhi.`,
    },
  ],

  lesson_10: [
    // Stage 1: The War of Attrition (1969–1970)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[1.1]</span> In **March 1969**, Egyptian President Gamal Abdel Nasser officially renounced the 1967 ceasefire and launched the **War of Attrition** (**Harb al-Istinzaf**) along the Suez Canal. Recognizing that Egypt lacked the offensive air power to cross the canal, Nasser aimed to exploit Israel’s acute sensitivity to military casualties by subjecting the Bar-Lev Line to unrelenting artillery barrages, commando ambushes, and sniper fire.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[1.2]</span> Israel responded with deep-penetration bombing raids into the Nile Delta using newly acquired American **F-4 Phantom jets**, forcing Nasser to turn to Moscow for emergency assistance. The Soviet Union deployed over **15,000 Soviet military advisers** and advanced **SAM-2 and SAM-3 surface-to-air missile batteries** along the canal. When US Secretary of State William Rogers brokered a ceasefire in **August 1970**, Egypt immediately violated the agreement by rolling missile batteries right up to the canal, creating an umbrella that would prove decisive in 1973.`,
    },
    // Stage 2: Sadat Expels Soviet Advisers (1971–1972)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[2.1]</span> Following Nasser’s sudden death in September 1970, his successor **Anwar Sadat** inherited a bankrupt Egyptian economy, an exhausted population, and a frozen diplomatic landscape. Sadat recognized that a permanent state of "no war, no peace" was destroying Egypt. In 1971, he made secret diplomatic overtures to the United States offering peace in exchange for Israeli withdrawal from Sinai, but was rebuffed by Israeli Prime Minister Golda Meir and US National Security Adviser **Henry Kissinger**.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[2.2]</span> Frustrated by Soviet reluctance to supply long-range offensive strike aircraft and suspicious of superpower détente, Sadat stunned the world in **July 1972** by abruptly expelling all **15,000 Soviet military advisers** and technicians from Egypt. Western and Israeli intelligence tragically misread this move as a sign of Egyptian military weakness and disarray; in reality, Sadat was eliminating Soviet interference to clear the path for an independent military offensive.`,
    },
    // Stage 3: The Surprise Attack on Yom Kippur (6 October 1973)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[3.1]</span> At 14:00 on Saturday, **6 October 1973**, Egypt and Syria launched **Operation Badr**, a coordinated two-front surprise assault. They struck on **Yom Kippur** (the Day of Atonement), the holiest day in Judaism, when Israeli radio was silent, transportation was halted, and military units were operating with skeleton crews. In the north, three Syrian infantry divisions and 800 tanks smashed into Israeli lines on the **Golan Heights**, outnumbering the defenders eleven-to-one.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[3.2]</span> In the south, 80,000 Egyptian troops crossed the Suez Canal under cover of a ferocious 2,000-gun artillery bombardment. Using high-pressure water cannons mounted on pontoons, Egyptian engineering teams blasted **60 massive breaches** through the 20-metre sand rampart of the Bar-Lev Line in less than ten hours. Egyptian infantry armed with portable Soviet **Sagger wire-guided anti-tank missiles** and mobile **SAM batteries** decimated counter-attacking Israeli tanks and jets, establishing a solid six-mile beachhead in Sinai.`,
    },
    // Stage 4: The US Emergency Airlift & Israeli Counter-Attack (12–16 October 1973)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[4.1]</span> By 11 October, Israel had halted the Syrian advance on the Golan in desperate tank battles at the "Valley of Tears" and pushed Syrian forces back beyond the 1967 line. However, the IDF faced an existential crisis in Sinai, having lost hundreds of tanks and aircraft. On **14 October**, US President Richard Nixon ordered **Operation Nickel Grass**—a massive emergency military airlift flying 22,000 tons of tanks, artillery, ammunition, and electronic jamming pods directly into Israel to counter a concurrent Soviet airlift to Egypt and Syria.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[4.2]</span> On **15–16 October**, General **Ariel Sharon** exploited a gap between the Egyptian Second and Third Armies at Deversoir. Sharon’s division ferried a pontoon bridge across the Suez Canal onto the Egyptian mainland, destroyed Egyptian SAM missile sites, and encircled the **Egyptian Third Army** (20,000 soldiers) in the desert. With Israeli tanks within 100 kilometres of Cairo and Soviet Premier Brezhnev threatening direct military intervention, the superpowers faced their tensest nuclear standoff since the Cuban Missile Crisis.`,
    },
    // Stage 5: The Arab Oil Embargo & Ceasefire (October 1973)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[5.1]</span> On **17 October 1973**, the Arab members of OPEC (Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries), led by King Faisal of Saudi Arabia, deployed the "oil weapon." They declared a total oil embargo against the United States, the Netherlands, and other allies of Israel, while cutting overall production by 5% each month. The price of crude oil quadrupled from \$3 to nearly **\$12 per barrel**, causing fuel rationing, skyrocketing inflation, and severe economic stagflation across the Western world.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[5.2]</span> The oil embargo transformed global geopolitics overnight, proving that Western economies were critically vulnerable to Arab economic leverage. Under intense superpower pressure, the UN Security Council passed **Resolution 338**, bringing a definitive ceasefire on **25 October 1973**. The Yom Kippur War shattered the myth of Israeli invincibility, claimed the lives of 2,600 Israeli and 15,000 Arab soldiers, and forced Washington to realize that Middle Eastern diplomacy could no longer be frozen.`,
    },
  ],

  lesson_11: [
    // Stage 1: Kissinger’s Shuttle Diplomacy (1974–1975)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[1.1]</span> The 1973 oil shock demonstrated that Middle Eastern stability was a vital American national security priority. To prevent renewed hostilities and exclude the Soviet Union from regional diplomacy, US Secretary of State **Henry Kissinger** launched an unprecedented campaign of "**Shuttle Diplomacy**." Flying tirelessly between Cairo, Damascus, and Jerusalem aboard his Boeing 707, Kissinger pursued an incremental, step-by-step negotiating strategy rather than attempting an impossible comprehensive peace conference.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[1.2]</span> In January 1974, Kissinger brokered the **Sinai I Disengagement Agreement**, which separated Egyptian and Israeli forces, allowed the trapped Egyptian Third Army to be resupplied, and saw Israel withdraw 20 kilometres from the Suez Canal, allowing Egypt to clear and reopen the canal in June 1975. In May 1974, Kissinger achieved a disengagement accord between Israel and Syria on the Golan Heights, followed by the **Sinai II Accord** in September 1975, which returned the vital Abu Rudeis oil fields to Egypt.`,
    },
    // Stage 2: Sadat’s Historic Visit to Jerusalem (November 1977)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[2.1]</span> In **May 1977**, Israeli politics experienced an earthquake when the right-wing **Likud party** won a stunning general election, ending 29 years of uninterrupted Labour rule. Prime Minister **Menachem Begin**, a former Irgun underground leader, believed passionately in the historic "Greater Land of Israel" (Eretz Yisrael) and seemed an impossible partner for Arab peace. Yet Egyptian President **Anwar Sadat** faced catastrophic domestic food riots in Cairo and an economy drained by decades of military spending.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[2.2]</span> On **9 November 1977**, Sadat stunned the world by declaring to the Egyptian parliament that he was prepared to travel "to the ends of the earth, even to the Knesset itself" to prevent further bloodshed. Ten days later, on **19 November 1977**, Sadat landed at Ben-Gurion Airport and became the first Arab leader to set foot in the Jewish state. Addressing the Israeli Knesset in Jerusalem, Sadat offered full diplomatic recognition and permanent peace in exchange for the complete return of the occupied Sinai Peninsula and self-determination for Palestinians.`,
    },
    // Stage 3: The Camp David Summit (September 1978)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[3.1]</span> When direct bilateral negotiations between Begin and Sadat deadlocked over Israeli settlements in Sinai and Palestinian autonomy, US President **Jimmy Carter** took the immense political gamble of inviting both leaders to the secluded presidential retreat at **Camp David**, Maryland. For thirteen agonizing days from **5 to 17 September 1978**, Carter engaged in marathon mediation, shuttling between Begin’s and Sadat’s cabins when the two men refused to speak to each other after day three.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[3.2]</span> Carter rescued the summit from collapse by threatening to terminate US foreign aid and personally appealing to Begin’s concern for his grandchildren's future. On **17 September 1978**, the summit concluded with the signing of the **Camp David Accords**, which established two frameworks: a bilateral framework for a peace treaty returning the entire Sinai Peninsula to Egypt in exchange for diplomatic recognition, and a vague framework for Palestinian autonomy in the West Bank and Gaza Strip.`,
    },
    // Stage 4: The Egypt-Israel Peace Treaty (26 March 1979)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[4.1]</span> On **26 March 1979**, on the North Lawn of the White House, Menachem Begin and Anwar Sadat signed the historic **Egypt-Israel Peace Treaty**, witnessed by Jimmy Carter. Under the treaty, Israel agreed to completely evacuate the Sinai Peninsula in phases over three years, including dismantling twelve Jewish settlements (most notably Yamit) and returning the Sinai airfields to Egyptian sovereignty.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[4.2]</span> In return, Egypt became the very first Arab nation to officially recognize the State of Israel, ending a 31-year state of war, establishing full diplomatic relations, exchanging ambassadors, opening borders, and guaranteeing unimpeded Israeli passage through the Suez Canal and Straits of Tiran. To cement the peace, the United States guaranteed billions of dollars in annual military and economic aid to both nations, transforming Egypt into a cornerstone of American regional strategy.`,
    },
    // Stage 5: Arab Boycott & The Assassination of Sadat (1979–1981)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[5.1]</span> While celebrated in the West, Sadat’s separate peace provoked incandescent fury across the Arab world. Arab governments condemned Sadat as a traitor who had sold out the Palestinian cause to reclaim Egyptian desert sand. The Arab League expelled Egypt from its membership, relocated its headquarters from Cairo to Tunis, severed diplomatic ties, and enacted a total economic boycott against Egyptian trade.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[5.2]</span> Within Egypt, Islamic fundamentalist groups organized fierce domestic opposition. On **6 October 1981**, during the annual military parade in Cairo celebrating the eighth anniversary of the Yom Kippur canal crossing, Islamic Jihad militants led by Lieutenant **Khalid Islambouli** jumped from an artillery truck, hurling grenades and firing assault rifles into the reviewing stand. **Anwar Sadat was assassinated**, paying with his life for the peace treaty, but his successor **Hosni Mubarak** chose to honor the treaty, preserving the cold peace with Israel.`,
    },
  ],

  lesson_12: [
    // Stage 1: The Israeli Invasion of Lebanon (June 1982)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[1.1]</span> Following their expulsion from Jordan in 1970–71, the PLO transformed southern Lebanon into an armed redoubt known as "**Fatahland**," launching Katyusha rocket barrages and cross-border raids into northern Israel. In March 1978, Israel launched **Operation Litani**, occupying southern Lebanon up to the Litani River before withdrawing in favor of UN peacekeepers (**UNIFIL**) and a proxy Christian militia, the South Lebanon Army (SLA).`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[1.2]</span> On **3 June 1982**, the rogue Abu Nidal terrorist faction critically wounded Israeli Ambassador **Shlomo Argov** in London. Although Abu Nidal was a sworn enemy of Arafat, Israeli Defence Minister **Ariel Sharon** seized the pretext to launch **Operation Peace for Galilee** on **6 June 1982**. Sharon deceived Prime Minister Begin and the Israeli Cabinet, claiming the invasion would advance only 40 kilometres to clear rocket range, but instead drove armoured divisions 100 kilometres north to lay siege to the Lebanese capital of **Beirut**.`,
    },
    // Stage 2: The Sabra and Shatila Massacres (September 1982)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[2.1]</span> For ten brutal weeks in the summer of 1982, the IDF bombarded West Beirut with heavy artillery and airstrikes, cutting off water and electricity to force the surrender of 15,000 PLO fighters. In August, US mediator Philip Habib negotiated the evacuation of the PLO leadership and guerrillas by sea to **Tunis**, leaving Palestinian civilian refugee camps in Beirut under international protection guarantees.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[2.2]</span> On 14 September, Lebanon’s newly elected Christian President, **Bachir Gemayel**, was assassinated. In response, Sharon ordered the IDF into West Beirut and authorized Christian Phalangist (**Kataeb**) militias to enter the **Sabra and Shatila refugee camps** to "mop up terrorists." Over three horrific days from **16 to 18 September 1982**, while Israeli troops fired illumination flares over the camps and prevented exits, Phalangist militants slaughtered between **800 and 3,500 unarmed Palestinian refugees**, mostly women, children, and elderly men.`,
    },
    // Stage 3: Outbreak of the First Intifada (December 1987)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[3.1]</span> The Sabra and Shatila massacre provoked unprecedented moral outrage within Israel: over **400,000 citizens** rallied in Tel Aviv demanding an investigation. The official **Kahan Commission of Inquiry** concluded that Defence Minister Ariel Sharon bore "personal responsibility" for failing to foresee the bloodshed, forcing his resignation. Israel gradually withdrew into a narrow "Security Zone" in southern Lebanon, leaving behind a power vacuum quickly filled by a new, radical Iranian-backed Shiite militia: **Hezbollah**.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[3.2]</span> On **8 December 1987**, in the Jabalya refugee camp in the Gaza Strip, an Israeli army tank transporter crashed into four Palestinian civilian cars, killing four laborers. Rumors spread rapidly that the collision was a deliberate revenge attack for the stabbing of an Israeli salesman. Within hours, spontaneous mass riots erupted across Gaza and spread like wildfire to the West Bank, marking the explosion of the **First Intifada** ("shaking off").`,
    },
    // Stage 4: The Israeli Response & The Rise of Hamas (1987–1988)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[4.1]</span> The First Intifada was a genuine grassroots rebellion led not by the distant PLO in Tunis, but by local Palestinian youth—the "children of the stones"—and coordinated by the clandestine **Unified National Leadership of the Uprising** (**UNLU**). Palestinians engaged in general strikes, tax boycotts, barricades, and stone-throwing demonstrations. Israeli Defence Minister **Yitzhak Rabin** responded with an "Iron Fist" policy, directing troops to use "force, might, and beatings" to crush dissent, but global television broadcasts of armed soldiers clubbing teenage demonstrators severely damaged Israel’s international image.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[4.2]</span> The uprising transformed Palestinian politics by giving birth to **Hamas** (the Islamic Resistance Movement), founded in December 1987 by **Sheikh Ahmed Yassin** as an offshoot of the Muslim Brotherhood. Unlike the secular PLO, Hamas’s 1988 Charter rejected any diplomatic compromise, framed the conflict as an immutable religious holy war (**jihad**), and demanded the total liberation of historic Palestine from the Jordan River to the Mediterranean Sea, directly challenging Arafat's authority.`,
    },
    // Stage 5: Arafat Renounces Terrorism & Accepts Resolution 242 (December 1988)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[5.1]</span> Terrified of being permanently sidelined by Hamas and the homegrown leaders of the Intifada, Yasser Arafat executed a historic diplomatic pivot. In **November 1988** in Algiers, the Palestinian National Council proclaimed the **Declaration of Independence of the State of Palestine**, implicitly accepting the 1947 UN partition and acknowledging the reality of Israel’s existence.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[5.2]</span> On **13 December 1988**, speaking before a special session of the UN General Assembly in **Geneva** (after the US denied him a visa for New York), Arafat took the decisive step: he explicitly accepted **UN Resolutions 242 and 338**, recognized Israel’s right to exist in peace and security, and completely renounced all forms of terrorism. The United States immediately recognized Arafat's declaration, opening formal diplomatic dialogue with the PLO for the first time in history.`,
    },
  ],

  lesson_13: [
    // Stage 1: The Madrid Peace Conference (October 1991)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[1.1]</span> The early 1990s witnessed revolutionary global geopolitical shifts that opened a window for Middle Eastern peace. The **collapse of the Soviet Union** (1989–1991) left the United States as the sole global superpower and cut off Syrian and Palestinian access to Soviet military and financial sponsorship. Concurrently, Yasser Arafat made the catastrophic blunder of backing Iraqi dictator **Saddam Hussein** during the **1990–1991 Gulf War**, prompting Kuwait and Gulf monarchies to expel 300,000 Palestinian workers and cut off hundreds of millions of dollars in financial aid to the PLO.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[1.2]</span> Exploiting American prestige following the Gulf War victory, US President **George H.W. Bush** and Secretary of State **James Baker** pressured Israeli Prime Minister Yitzhak Shamir and Arab states to attend the historic **Madrid Peace Conference** in **October 1991**. Co-chaired with Mikhail Gorbachev, Madrid brought Israeli, Syrian, Lebanese, and a joint Jordanian-Palestinian delegation into face-to-face bilateral negotiations for the first time, establishing the principle that peace was attainable.`,
    },
    // Stage 2: The Secret Oslo Negotiations (1993)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[2.1]</span> While the formal, public Madrid bilateral talks in Washington deadlocked over procedural formalities and Shamir's refusal to compromise, Israeli politics changed dramatically in June 1992 with the election of **Yitzhak Rabin’s** Labour government. In early 1993, Israeli academics Yair Hirschfeld and Ron Pundak, backed by Deputy Foreign Minister **Yossi Beilin**, opened a secret back-channel in **Oslo**, Norway, mediated by Norwegian diplomat Terje Rød-Larsen and Foreign Minister Johan Jørgen Holst.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[2.2]</span> In secluded Norwegian estates, Israeli negotiators met face-to-face with senior PLO official **Ahmed Qurei** (Abu Ala). Away from media leaks and political posturing, the negotiators drafted an astonishing compromise: in the **Letters of Mutual Recognition** (9 September 1993), Yasser Arafat officially recognized Israel's right to exist in peace and renounced terrorism, while Yitzhak Rabin officially recognized the PLO as the legitimate representative of the Palestinian people.`,
    },
    // Stage 3: The Oslo I Accord & The White House Handshake (13 September 1993)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[3.1]</span> On **13 September 1993**, on the South Lawn of the White House, President **Bill Clinton** hosted the historic signing of the **Declaration of Principles on Interim Self-Government Arrangements** (**Oslo I**). Before 3,000 dignitaries and a global television audience, Yitzhak Rabin and Yasser Arafat exchanged a hesitant, historic handshake, sealing an agreement to end decades of existential conflict.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[3.2]</span> Oslo I established the "**Gaza-Jericho First**" formula: Israel agreed to withdraw its military forces from the Gaza Strip and the West Bank town of Jericho, transferring civil administration to a newly formed **Palestinian Authority** (PA) headed by Arafat. A five-year interim period was established during which permanent status issues—including the status of Jerusalem, Palestinian refugees, Jewish settlements, and final borders—would be resolved through future negotiations.`,
    },
    // Stage 4: Israel-Jordan Peace Treaty & Oslo II (1994–1995)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[4.1]</span> The breakthrough of Oslo I unleashed immense regional momentum. On **26 October 1994**, King Hussein of Jordan and Yitzhak Rabin signed the **Israel-Jordan Peace Treaty** in the Arava desert, witnessed by President Clinton, making Jordan the second Arab state to normalize relations with Israel. In September 1995, Rabin and Arafat signed the **Oslo II Interim Agreement** in Taba, Egypt, expanding Palestinian self-rule across the major population centres of the West Bank.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[4.2]</span> Oslo II divided the West Bank into three complex administrative jurisdictions: **Area A** (full Palestinian civil and security control, covering major Arab cities); **Area B** (Palestinian civil control with joint Israeli military security control, covering rural villages); and **Area C** (exclusive Israeli civil and military control, covering 60% of the West Bank, all Jewish settlements, bypass roads, and the Jordan Valley). This division left Palestinian territory fragmented into disconnected enclaves.`,
    },
    // Stage 5: Extremist Violence & The Assassination of Rabin (4 November 1995)
    {
      p1: `<span class="para-ref">[5.1]</span> The peace process provoked violent resistance from ideological rejectionists on both fringes. In February 1994, Jewish extremist Baruch Goldstein massacred 29 Muslim worshippers in Hebron’s Cave of the Patriarchs. In response, **Hamas and Palestinian Islamic Jihad** unleashed a relentless campaign of suicide bombings against Israeli civilian buses in Tel Aviv and Jerusalem, killing dozens and eroding Israeli public faith in the peace process. Concurrently, right-wing Israeli politicians, including Likud leader **Benjamin Netanyahu**, held rallies depicting Rabin in a Nazi SS uniform, denouncing the handover of biblical land as treason.`,
      p2: `<span class="para-ref">[5.2]</span> On the evening of **4 November 1995**, over 100,000 Israelis gathered at Kings of Israel Square in Tel Aviv for a massive rally supporting the peace process, singing "Shir LaShalom" (Song for Peace) alongside Rabin. As Prime Minister Rabin walked toward his car, **Yigal Amir**, a 25-year-old Jewish religious extremist who believed Rabin was betraying God’s land, shot him twice in the back. **Yitzhak Rabin’s assassination** dealt a devastating blow to the peace process, shattering national unity and leaving the dream of a permanent Middle Eastern resolution frozen in tragic deadlock.`,
    },
  ],
};
